# AgentXcelerate-Hackathon Global AI Rules

## 1. Project Context & Stack
- Project Name: Autonomous Supply Chain Ops (AgentXcelerate)
- Architecture: Event-Driven Multi-Agent Architecture (Async Orchestrator + Event Bus)
- Python Version: 3.10+
- Primary Libraries: FastAPI, Streamlit, Pydantic (v2), google-genai, asyncio, requests
- Key Goal: Hackathon MVP focused on speed, modularity, and explicit inter-agent logging.

## 2. Directory Structure Conventions
Keep all code strictly inside assigned modular subdirectories:
- `agents/`: Individual agent logic files (`diagnostic_agent.py`, `inventory_agent.py`, `procurement_agent.py`, `logistics_agent.py`)
- `core/`: Event Bus, state schemas, and central orchestration logic (`event_bus.py`, `orchestrator.py`, `schemas.py`)
- `frontend/`: Streamlit dashboard and backend client (`app.py`, `api_client.py`)
- `mocks/`: Mock database definitions and vendor API wrappers (`suppliers.py`, `inventory_db.py`)
- `tests/`: Isolated unit/integration scripts per component

## 3. Communication & Schema Rules
- EVERY inter-agent message MUST adhere to the `AgentEvent` Pydantic structure containing:
  `trace_id` (str), `timestamp` (ISO str), `sender_agent` (str), `recipient_agent` (str), `event_type` (str), `data` (dict).
- Do NOT use raw, unstructured strings for agent-to-agent communication. Use JSON-serializable structures or Pydantic models.

## 4. Code Style & Quality Standards
- Async First: Use standard `async/await` patterns for network requests, mock delays, and agent operations.
- Explicit Typing: Include Python type hints (`str`, `dict`, `List`, `Optional`) on all functions.
- Error Handling: Fail gracefully with standard logging—never allow uncaught exceptions to crash the FastAPI/Streamlit processes.
- Documentation: Keep inline comments concise and functional. No verbose boilerplate.

## 5. AI Assistant Behavior Constraints
- Do NOT rewrite entire codebases when asked to make small changes. Modify only the targeted functions. DONOT CHNAGE THE BASE ARCHITECTURE.
- Respect environment isolation—never leak API key placeholders into code files (always source from `os.getenv`).
- Avoid introducing unrequested third-party dependencies beyond `requirements.txt`.
